<div class="section" style="padding-top:5rem;max-width:700px">
  <div class="section-tag">// security</div>
  <h1 style="font-size:2.25rem">How your data is protected</h1>
  <p style="color:var(--dim);margin-top:1rem;line-height:1.7">
    We built NoteFlame so that even we can't read your secrets. This page explains exactly how that works.
    If something here doesn't make sense, that's a bug in our explanation, not a feature.
  </p>

  <div style="margin-top:3rem">
    <h2>Encryption</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.7">
      Every drop is encrypted with <strong style="color:var(--text)">AES-256-GCM</strong> before it's written to the database.
      The encryption key is derived from a random slug using PBKDF2 (100,000 iterations, SHA-256).
      The slug itself is never stored &mdash; only a SHA-256 hash of it, used for lookups.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <h2>What we store vs. what we don't</h2>
    <div style="margin-top:1.25rem;display:grid;grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;gap:1px;border:1px solid var(--border);background:var(--border)">
      <div style="background:var(--bg);padding:1.25rem">
        <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.65rem;color:var(--green);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.1em;margin-bottom:.75rem">Stored in our database</div>
        <ul style="list-style:none;color:var(--dim);font-size:.85rem;line-height:2">
          <li>SHA-256 hash of the slug (for lookup)</li>
          <li>Encrypted ciphertext (unreadable without key)</li>
          <li>IV and auth tag (needed for decryption)</li>
          <li>Metadata: view count, expiry, password hash</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div style="background:var(--bg);padding:1.25rem">
        <div style="font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.65rem;color:var(--red);text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.1em;margin-bottom:.75rem">Never stored anywhere</div>
        <ul style="list-style:none;color:var(--dim);font-size:.85rem;line-height:2">
          <li>The raw slug (exists only in the URL)</li>
          <li>The decryption key (derived at read time)</li>
          <li>The plaintext content</li>
          <li>Server-side logs of content</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <h2>The encryption flow</h2>
    <div style="margin-top:1.25rem;border:1px solid var(--border);padding:1.5rem;font-family:var(--mono);font-size:.78rem;color:var(--dim);line-height:2.2">
      <div><span style="color:var(--muted)">1.</span> <span style="color:var(--text)">slug</span> = random 22-char string (URL-safe base64)</div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--muted)">2.</span> <span style="color:var(--text)">slugHash</span> = SHA-256(<span style="color:var(--text)">slug</span>) <span style="color:var(--muted)">&rarr; stored for lookup</span></div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--muted)">3.</span> <span style="color:var(--text)">key</span> = PBKDF2(<span style="color:var(--text)">slug</span>, ENCRYPTION_SECRET, 100k iterations)</div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--muted)">4.</span> <span style="color:var(--text)">iv</span> = random 12 bytes</div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--muted)">5.</span> <span style="color:var(--text)">ciphertext</span>, <span style="color:var(--text)">authTag</span> = AES-256-GCM.encrypt(<span style="color:var(--text)">plaintext</span>, <span style="color:var(--text)">key</span>, <span style="color:var(--text)">iv</span>)</div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--muted)">6.</span> Store: <span style="color:var(--text)">slugHash</span>, <span style="color:var(--text)">ciphertext</span>, <span style="color:var(--text)">iv</span>, <span style="color:var(--text)">authTag</span></div>
      <div><span style="color:var(--muted)">7.</span> Return URL: /d/<span style="color:var(--lime)">slug</span> <span style="color:var(--muted)">&rarr; slug is only in the URL, never stored</span></div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <h2>On read</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.7">
      When someone opens the link, we hash the slug from the URL, look up the record, re-derive the key,
      decrypt the content, serve it, then either increment the view counter or permanently delete the record.
      The plaintext exists in server memory for the duration of that single request. It's never written to disk or logged.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <h2>Password protection</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.7">
      If you set a password on a drop, the viewer must enter it before decryption happens.
      The password is hashed with bcrypt (cost factor 10) and compared server-side. Wrong password = no decryption attempt.
      This gives you a second channel: share the link in Slack, send the password via text. Neither is useful alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <h2>What a database breach reveals</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.7">
      If someone dumped our entire database tomorrow, they'd get: encrypted blobs, slug hashes (not slugs),
      IVs, and auth tags. Without the original slugs (which only existed in URLs we never stored), the
      encryption keys can't be re-derived. The ciphertext is computationally useless.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <h2>What we don't do</h2>
    <div style="margin-top:.75rem;color:var(--dim);font-size:.9rem;line-height:1.8">
      <div>We don't read your drops. We architecturally <em>can't</em>.</div>
      <div>We don't log plaintext content.</div>
      <div>We don't sell data. There are no analytics trackers on the view page.</div>
      <div>We don't store IPs in plaintext &mdash; they're hashed if recorded at all.</div>
      <div>We have never received a legal request for user data.</div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:2.5rem">
    <h2>Limitations</h2>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.7">
      This is not end-to-end encrypted in the Signal sense. Decryption happens on our server, not in your browser.
      That means you're trusting that our server code does what we say it does. We plan to open-source the codebase
      so you can verify.
    </p>
    <p style="color:var(--dim);margin-top:.75rem;line-height:1.7">
      If your threat model requires client-side-only decryption, NoteFlame isn't the right tool.
      For sharing passwords with coworkers who would otherwise paste them in Slack, it's a meaningful improvement.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div style="margin-top:3rem;padding:1.5rem;border:1px solid var(--border);text-align:center">
    <p style="color:var(--dim);font-size:.85rem">Questions about our security model?</p>
    <p style="color:var(--muted);font-size:.78rem;margin-top:.35rem">Reach out and we'll answer publicly. Transparency is the point.</p>
  </div>
</div>
